{
  "about": "The app ABI's role table: one row per verb an app reaches through its AppContext (native/moy_app/README.md). role and verb name it; server is where it is served: c (a C function in this module taking the grant) or shell (the Python console, through the server it registers for the role; a compiled app reaches it through the ROLE upcall) or proc:<name> (a process's, the cart moybyte.<name>: the console registers its link to the process for the role, as runtime/system/achievements.py's NotifyRole for proc:achievements, and the upcall reaches it as a shell row's); grant is the manifest permission that hands the role to a user app, or never; wasm is the import's type as WAMR's signature string (a compiled app imports the row as <role>_<verb> from module moybyte.app, native/moy_app/moy_app_wasm.h), or null where a compiled app cannot import it. ROLES, the bindings and docs/app_api_v1.md's table are held to it by tests. The ui role is the public UI API's rows for a compiled app (docs/ui_api_2026-10.md, native/moy_ui/moy_ui_wasm.h): served in C by native/moy_ui, taking no grant and imported by every compiled app; a Python app draws the same C through `import ui`, so no AppContext role object is built for it. permissions is the permission table (docs/os_2026-11/processes.md section 2): perm is the manifest name (files also matches files:<folder>); grants is floor, role:<role> (the rows above whose grant names this perm), api:<service>, boot, lease (lease:<row>, a raw lease of a row of the board's resource table), native (a native module's run: the one line full access to the console, which every native app asks for) or marker; tiers the cart runtimes that may hold it; prompt its line of the install prompt, full (full access to the row a lease names), or null where there is nothing to consent to; needs the console service it needs (lease: the board offers the row), or null; py the names a Python cart holds while it is granted, each None while Settings turns it off. runtime/shell/app_perms.py restates it and tests/test_app_perms.py holds both, and the role rows to the C allowlist.",
